 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Each refinement step involves fitting a quadratic polynomial (constant time for fixed number of points per cluster, but depends on cluster size) and computing residuals. In the worst case, with $N$ points and $K$ clusters, fitting is $O(N)$ per cluster. The total complexity depends on the convergence rate to the error bound.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the points and values, plus $O(K)$ for the cluster hierarchy.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Performance:** Typically produces a lower number of clusters than PCA-based splitting for the same approximation error, particularly when the function is curved in only one direction, resulting in long and skinny clusters [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient triangulation of scattered data; better representation of smooth surfaces via quadratic polynomials compared to piecewise linear; data-dependent hierarchy.
-*   **Limitations:** Not invariant under affine transformations (unless the specific affine-invariant variant is used, which has its own pitfalls); requires 2D plane assumption (generalization to higher dimensions requires local projection via PCA) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When approximating smooth surfaces from scattered 2D data with a strict error bound and minimizing cluster count is critical.
-*   **When not to use:** When affine invariance is strictly required and data is not densely sampled/smooth; when 3D volumetric data is processed directly without projection.
